<BaseLayout title="Paint scratch assessment">
  <h1>Paint scratch assessment</h1>
  <p>For a scratch on a painted metal panel, start with a careful wash and dry. This removes dirt that can make a mark look deeper than it is.</p>
  <h2>Assess the damage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the mark transfers to a clean microfiber towel with an appropriate paint-safe cleaner, it may be foreign paint or surface contamination.</li>
    <li>If the mark remains but does not catch a fingernail, it may be within the clear coat.</li>
    <li>If a fingernail catches, the scratch may be deeper than the clear coat. Polishing alone may not remove it safely.</li>
    <li>If primer or bare metal is visible, protect the area from corrosion and seek a suitable paint repair.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use the factory paint code to select matching touch-up materials. Avoid aggressive abrasion or machine polishing until the depth and condition of the finish are understood.</p>
</BaseLayout>
